 "answer": "Probably not by much. The latest forecast I found says tomorrow will still be bad, though it may improve a little.\n\n- **PSI forecast:** NEA's 24-hour PSI forecast for tomorrow is 150 to 220, which is unhealthy to very unhealthy. That's about the same as the latest readings. The 24-hour PSI was 159–222 as of 8pm in NEA's 16 October update.\n- **Why it might ease:** Showers are expected over Singapore and the surrounding region. Winds are forecast to shift to blow from the south-southeast or south, which could reduce the smoke coming from Kalimantan.\n- **Why it might not:** Some smoke from Sumatra may still drift over, and the fires are still burning.\n- **Hourly readings:** These can change quickly. Afternoon showers helped keep PM2.5 from reaching the \"very high\" band today, but readings were still high in the south, central and west.\n\n**What to do**\n- Plan on minimal strenuous outdoor activity tomorrow.\n- Elderly people, pregnant women and children should minimise time outdoors. People with chronic heart or lung conditions should avoid outdoor activity.\n- Before you go out, check the 1-hour PM2.5 reading on the NEA website or the myENV app. NEA says to use that for immediate plans and the 24-hour PSI for planning ahead.\n- If PM2.5 is in the \"high\" band, avoid all outdoor activity for the next hour and consider wearing a mask.\n\nThe news sources I found were dated 16–17 October 2026. If today is a later date, check NEA's latest haze update for the current forecast.\n\nSources: NEA Haze Situation Update (16 Oct 2026) and Malay Mail (17 Oct 2026).",
